Planex multi_perception — multiple denotations of same state
================================================================
Validates: same Estimates -> multiple Perception functions

Setup:
  Estimate count = 0
  Estimate doubled (derived) = 0
  Closure inc registered

Registered 4 perceptions of the same state:
  - visual_text (visual rendering)
  - a11y_text (screen reader text)
  - json_snapshot (testing / serialization)
  - log_line (debugging)

Interaction: trigger inc 5 times

After interactions, the FOUR denotations of state (count=5, doubled=10):

  [visual]      Count: 5   (doubled: 10)
  [a11y]        Counter is at 5. Doubled value is 10.
  [json]        {"count": 5, "doubled": 10, "confidence": 1.00}
  [log]         [LOG] t=14573219 count=5 doubled=10

Independence test: free the a11y perception, others unaffected:
  Perception count: 3 (was 4, now 3)
  visual: Count: 5   (doubled: 10)
  json:   {"count": 5, "doubled": 10, "confidence": 1.00}
  log:    [LOG] t=14573219 count=5 doubled=10

Validation:
  Final count = 5 (expected 5) ✓
  Final doubled = 10 (expected 10) ✓
  Perceptions remaining = 3 (expected 3) ✓

=== Demo complete ===

What this demonstrates:
  1. FOUR perceptions coexist for the same Estimates
     - visual (screen pixels in a real app)
     - a11y (screen reader text)
     - json (testing / serialization / undo snapshots)
     - log (debugging)
  2. Each perception is independent — removing one doesn't affect others
  3. Each perception is a pure function (same input -> same output)
  4. Different perceptions can produce different output types

Why this matters:
  Mainstream UI libraries (React, SwiftUI, Flutter) have ONE render
  path — you'd have to special-case each denotation inside a single
  render function, or maintain parallel trees (DOM + a11y tree + ...).
  Planex's Perception abstraction makes multiple denotations first-class.

This is the strongest argument for Perception as a 4th abstraction.
Without it, multi-denotation UI is awkward. With it, it's natural.
